---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import blogStudyUsa from '../assets/blog/study-usa.jpg';
const title = "Studying in the USA: What to Know Before Applying | CloudBridge";
const description = "US tuition costs, the F-1 visa process, OPT, and scholarships — a complete guide to studying in the USA from CloudBridge.";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<section class="py-10 bg-white border-b border-black/5">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
    <p class="crumb !text-ink/40"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blogs">Blogs</a> / Study in the USA</p>
  </div>
</section>
<article class="py-10 md:py-14">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="course-badge">Study Destinations</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-bridge-950 leading-tight">Studying in the USA: What Bangladeshi Students Should Know Before Applying</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/50">Updated July 2026 · 9 min read</p>
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <Image src={blogStudyUsa} alt="A map of the United States with a small American flag marker, representing study destinations across the USA" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" widths={[480, 768, 1200]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 700px, 100vw" />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">The USA has more universities than almost anywhere else in the world — over 4,000 of them — which is both the appeal and the problem. It's genuinely possible to find a program that fits your budget, your grades, and your goals, but only if you know where to start looking. Here's a straight answer to the questions we get asked most: what it costs, how the application actually works, and what your visa options look like once you're in.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Why students still choose the USA</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">The flexibility of the American system is a big part of it. Most undergraduate degrees let you take courses across different subjects before formally declaring a major in your second year, so you're not locked into one path from day one. On the postgraduate side, the US is home to some of the world's most research-active universities, with strong funding and assistantship opportunities for students who want to combine study with paid research or teaching work.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Then there's OPT (Optional Practical Training) — up to 12 months of work authorisation after graduation, extended to 36 months for STEM graduates. For students in engineering, computer science, or data-related fields especially, that extended window to gain US work experience is often the deciding factor.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">What it actually costs</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Costs vary more in the US than almost anywhere else, which is exactly why "how much does it cost to study in the USA" doesn't have one honest answer. Public state universities typically charge international students somewhere between $10,000–$25,000 a year in tuition, while private universities can range from $25,000 up to $50,000+ depending on the institution's reputation and program. Community colleges are the most budget-friendly route — often $6,000–$12,000 a year — and a genuinely useful option if the plan is to complete two years there and transfer into a four-year university afterwards.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">On top of tuition, budget for living costs of roughly $800–$1,500 a month depending on the city and whether you're in on-campus housing or renting privately. Cities like New York or San Francisco sit well above that range; university towns in the Midwest or South are often noticeably cheaper.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-bridge-50 p-5 border border-bridge-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-bridge-900"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Ask about Graduate Assistantship positions if you're applying for a Master's — a teaching or research assistantship can cover a large part of your tuition and comes with a monthly stipend, but you often need to ask the department directly rather than wait for it to be advertised.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Application process and requirements</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Applications are made directly to each university, usually through their own portal or the Common App for undergraduates. You'll typically need academic transcripts, an English proficiency score (TOEFL or IELTS — many universities now also accept Duolingo English Test), a Statement of Purpose, letters of recommendation, and for some universities, SAT or GRE/GMAT scores — though a growing number of institutions have made standardised tests optional.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Once admitted, the university issues an I-20 form, which you'll need to pay the SEVIS fee and apply for your F-1 student visa. Unlike the UK, the US visa process includes an in-person interview at the embassy, so we spend real time with students preparing for exactly what gets asked and how to answer with confidence.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Scholarships and funding</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Merit scholarships at the undergraduate level and assistantships at the graduate level are the two biggest funding routes, and both are more accessible than most students assume — you just need a strong, honestly-written application and to apply to the right mix of universities. This is one of the areas where a second pair of eyes genuinely helps, since scholarship deadlines often fall earlier than the general admission deadline and are easy to miss if you're applying alone.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 border border-black/10 rounded-2xl divide-y divide-black/10 bg-white">
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Is the SAT or GRE always required?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Not always — a growing number of US universities have gone test-optional for both undergraduate and graduate admissions. It depends heavily on the specific university and program, so we check this case by case.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">How does the F-1 visa interview work?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">It's a short, in-person interview at the US Embassy where an officer asks about your study plans, finances, and intent to return home after your studies. We run mock interviews with students beforehand so nothing comes as a surprise.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Can I stay and work in the US after graduating?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Yes, through OPT — 12 months of work authorisation for most graduates, extended to 36 months total for STEM-eligible degrees.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-dusk-gradient text-white p-8 text-center">
      <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Weighing up the USA for your degree?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-white/75 text-sm max-w-md mx-auto">Talk to a CloudBridge counsellor about universities, costs, and your visa timeline — free, no obligation.</p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/#lead-form" class="btn-primary nav-link inline-flex">Get Free Consultation</a>
        <a href="/dest-usa" class="btn-secondary nav-link inline-flex">Explore USA Universities</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8"><a href="/blogs" class="nav-link text-sm font-semibold text-bridge-800 hover:underline">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>
  </div>
</article>

</Layout>
